You're on .NET 10, so the best approach is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already covers `File.*` and `Directory.*`.

### 1. Install packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # in your test project
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Read("/data/config.json"));

manager.Write("/data/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/out.txt"));

Assert.True(manager.DirectoryExists("/data"));
```

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` covers the entire `System.IO` surface, so you won't need to expand your interface every time you use a new file operation. The `MockFileSystem` gives you an in-memory filesystem for free — no mocking framework needed.
